### F069 · API versioning rules

- **Category:** Routing
- **Component(s):** Gateway, API Catalog
- **Summary:** Only the major version is specified in the URI; it is increased only for backward-incompatible changes. Minor version (not in URI) increases with new features; if instances have different minor versions, the service states the lowest minor version available (e.g. instance A v1.3+v2.2, instance B v1.2+v2.1 -> service provides v1.2 and v2.1). Patch version is not in URI and indicates doc patch/bug fix without API change. One instance must not provide two versions with the same major version. API Catalog differentiates full versions internally and returns docs for the highest version of the specified major version supported by all running services. API version is independent of product release. "Two last versions are supported."
- **Sources:** docs/user-guide/api-mediation/routing-requests-to-rest-apis.md; docs/user-guide/obtaining-information-about-api-services.md

### F070 · API versioning scheme and guidelines

- **Category:** Routing
- **Component(s):** Gateway, API Catalog
- **Summary:** APIs use semantic versioning; only the major version (`v1`, `v2`) appears in the URI and changes only for backward-incompatible changes. Minor version (not in URI) increases with new features; patch (not in URI) for doc patches/bug fixes. API version independent of product release; last two versions supported.
- **Constraints / notes:** A single service instance should not provide two versions with the same major version; an instance provides only one version, a separate instance can provide a different one. If a new resource exists in v1.2 the service must provide at least v1.2 (request fails on v1.1).
